family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">\$0.02 per image / \$0.025 per sec video</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">\sim 3\text{s} image / \sim 30\text{s} video</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Webhooks &amp; Async REST</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Permitted</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">API service capabilities may differ from base model features. For instance, while base model weights allow arbitrary sampler overrides and multi-LoRA stacking, hosted providers like fal.ai often restrict request payloads to standardized parameter subsets (e.g., single LoRA URL inputs, fixed frame rates of 5â€“24 FPS, and predefined resolution steps).</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">8. MCP, Skill, and Agent Integrations</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Autonomous visual generation requires exposing model execution endpoints to the master orchestrator (Hermes) via structured function calls. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides a standardized abstraction layer for node graph creation, queue management, parameter injection, and asset retrieval.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Under this agentic integration architecture, Hermes acts as the client agent, issuing standardized tool calls over Stdio or Streamable-HTTP transport interfaces to an MCP server instance, which converts agent directives into WebSocket or REST execution payloads for the underlying ComfyUI backend engine.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Several open-source MCP implementations support AI agent integration with visual generation engines:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">0xhackerfren/Comfy-UI-MCP</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: A comprehensive MCP server providing 57 specialized tools across 7 operational categories. It features 14 prebuilt workflow templates (covering txt2img, SDXL, FLUX, ControlNet, LoRAs, Wan video, and ACE Step audio). The server exposes node discovery (comfy_list_nodes), workflow graph generation (comfy_add_node, comfy_connect_nodes), dynamic execution (comfy_run_workflow), WebSocket progress tracking, and filesystem asset management.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">joenorton/comfyui-mcp-server</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: A lightweight MCP server built on a Streamable-HTTP transport layer. It features explicit asset identity tracking across execution sessions, persistent job status polling (get_job, get_queue_status), and an automated workflow iteration tool (regenerate) that replays previous node graphs with parameter overrides.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">samuraibuddha/mcp-comfyui</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: An API connector providing programmatically constructed workflow graphs, on-the-fly checkpoint swapping, queue clearing, image injection for img2img/ControlNet pipelines, and base64/filesystem image serialization.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">artokun/comfyui-mcp (DEPRECATED)</span><span>: Formerly a community-driven control plane, this repository is explicitly marked as </span><span style="font-weight:700">unmaintained</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. The Comfy-Org core team has transitioned to official agent and MCP tooling (Comfy Agent and Comfy MCP), superseding this project.</span></li></ul><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">If Hermes operates without a direct local ComfyUI instance, hosted APIs are invoked using standardized REST SDK wrappers (e.g., @fal-ai/serverless-client or Google Vertex AI SDKs) wrapped as native Hermes skills.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">9. Licensing and Commercial Use</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Licensing compliance is mandatory for assets generated for YouTube monetization. A dual-tier review evaluates both the model software/weights and the API service terms. Commercial safety is established by verifying four interconnected legal pillars: the specific model weight license (e.g., Apache 2.0 vs. non-commercial research restrictions), the underlying software implementation license (e.g., MIT, GPL), the governing hosted provider API terms, and final monetization compliance with YouTube content policies.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The model license taxonomy distinguishes between open commercial permissions, non-commercial research constraints, and API usage policies:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_4-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Apache 2.0 (Unrestricted Open Commercial)</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Wan 2.1 (all variants 1.3B, 14B, T2V, I2V) is distributed under the Apache 2.0 license. This permits royalty-free commercial distribution, model modification, self-hosting, and commercial monetization of output media without restrictions.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Non-Commercial / Research Restrictions</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Models such as FLUX.1-dev and certain open research checkpoints carry non-commercial visual use clauses. Deploying these models within a commercial YouTube production engine requires upgrading to FLUX.1-schnell (Apache 2.0) or licensing commercial API access from Black Forest Labs / fal.ai.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Commercial Output Rights via Managed APIs</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Vertex AI (Imagen 3, Veo 3.1) and fal.ai explicitly grant commercial ownership rights for generated outputs to the API caller. Content generated via these APIs contains no royalty obligations or geographic usage restrictions.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Watermarking and AI Transparency</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Google Imagen 3 embeds invisible pixel-level SynthID watermarks by default. These watermarks persist through common image transforms, providing verifiable proof of AI generation to satisfy YouTube's synthetic media disclosure policies.</span></li></ul><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Open-source model weight availability does not automatically imply commercial rights. Licenses must be validated for both the weight files and the execution code base prior to pipeline deployment.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">10. Cost, Latency, and Hardware Mechanics</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Production planning requires balancing hardware capital expenditure against per-unit API operational costs. Production cost dynamics strictly scale based on workload profiles: high-volume generation scenarios exceeding 10,000 images per day achieve superior unit cost efficiency via self-hosted hardware (such as dedicated H100 or RTX 4090 instances operating at ~$2.01/hr), whereas variable, lower-volume workloads under 50 assets per day eliminate idle compute costs entirely by leveraging serverless API pricing models.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">At $0.012 per megapixel image (e.g., FLUX.1-dev on fal.ai), 1,000 images incur $12.00 in cloud API costs. By comparison, hosting FLUX.2-dev on a dedicated cloud H100 PCIe instance ($2.01/hour) yields approximately 7 images per minute at FP8 precision. Processing 1,000 images requires roughly 143 GPU minutes, costing $4.79. The breakeven threshold occurs at approximately 200 images per day; beyond this volume, self-hosted or dedicated cloud GPUs provide greater cost efficiency.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">For video generation, self-hosting Wan 2.1 14B on an H100 PCIe instance takes roughly 11 minutes per 5-second clip unoptimized, costing ~$0.37 in raw compute. On fal.ai, the same clip generated via serverless Wan 2.1 I2V costs $0.20 (480p) or $0.40 (720p) with zero idle compute charges and zero infrastructure maintenance.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colsp